WB.L("coffee-bean-to-cup", "food", "beginner", "Coffee: from goat legend to flat white",
  "Every morning, billions of people drink the roasted seeds of a cherry from the Ethiopian highlands. Here is how that seed conquered the world, and what actually happens between the farm and your cup.",
  [
    ["Ethiopia, Yemen, two species", "Coffee has real roots in Ethiopia and Yemen, and two species, arabica and robusta, supply almost all the world's coffee.",
      "The tale of Kaldi, an Ethiopian goatherd whose goats pranced after eating red berries, is charming but only legend. It was first written down centuries after it supposedly happened. What we know is that arabica grows wild in Ethiopia's highland forests, and that the first solid evidence of people drinking coffee comes from fifteenth-century Yemen, where Sufi communities brewed it to stay awake for night prayers. It spread from the port of Mocha, whose name lives on in cafe menus.\n\nArabica is sweeter and more complex but fussy about heat. Robusta is hardier, more bitter and has roughly twice the caffeine, so it adds body and crema to espresso blends. Brazil leads production by far, with Vietnam usually second."],
    ["The social drink", "Coffeehouses turned coffee into a drink of trade and debate, and post-war migrants made Australia a nation of espresso drinkers.",
      "By the sixteenth century, coffeehouses thrived in Cairo and Istanbul, and some rulers tried to ban them, worried by the gossip and political talk. In seventeenth-century London they were nicknamed 'penny universities': for the price of a cup you could read newspapers and argue with strangers for hours. Merchants and ship owners met at Edward Lloyd's coffeehouse to swap shipping news, and it grew into Lloyd's of London. Unlike beer and wine, coffee sharpened people rather than dulling them.\n\nAustralia was a tea country, and coffee often meant instant, until Italian and Greek migrants opened cafes with espresso machines after the Second World War. The flat white followed, though New Zealanders claim it too."],
    ["From cherry to green bean", "A coffee bean is the seed of a fruit, and how the fruit is removed shapes the flavour.",
      "Coffee grows as small cherries that ripen from green to deep red. Each usually holds two seeds, the beans. After picking, the fruit must be removed, and the method matters. In washed processing, the skin and most of the sticky flesh are stripped off and microbes break down the rest, giving clean, bright flavours. In natural processing, the whole cherry is dried in the sun first, which can add fruity, wine-like notes. Either way you end up with a hard, pale green bean that smells grassy, nothing like coffee. Green beans keep well for months, so coffee travels the world in this state and is roasted closer to where it is drunk."],
    ["The magic of roasting", "Roasting creates coffee's flavour through browning reactions, and roasted beans go stale within weeks.",
      "Roasting is where flavour is created. Green beans tumble in hot air, usually reaching around 200 degrees Celsius. They dry out and turn yellow, smelling a little like bread. Then sugars caramelise, and amino acids react with sugars in the Maillard reaction, the same chemistry that browns toast and seared steak. Pressure builds until the beans crack audibly, rather like popcorn. Scientists have found hundreds of aroma compounds in roasted coffee.\n\nLight roasts keep more of the bean's origin character, often fruity or floral and more acidic. Dark roasts taste of the roast itself: smoky, bitter and chocolatey. Roasted beans go stale within weeks as aromas escape, so freshness matters."],
    ["Brewing is extraction", "Brewing dissolves flavour from the grounds, and under-extracted coffee tastes sour while over-extracted coffee tastes bitter.",
      "Whatever your method, brewing is extraction: water dissolves flavour from ground coffee. Acids dissolve first, then sweeter compounds, and bitter ones last. Too little extraction tastes sour and thin; too much turns harsh and bitter. Grind size, water temperature, contact time and the coffee-to-water ratio are your levers.\n\nEspresso forces hot water through very fine grounds at about nine bars for roughly half a minute, making a small, intense shot topped with crema. Plunger coffee steeps coarse grounds for several minutes. Cold brew steeps for many hours, giving a smoother, less acidic drink. Caffeine works by blocking adenosine, which makes us sleepy. This is general information, so check healthdirect for caffeine limits."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does robusta differ from arabica?", ["It is sweeter and more complex, but fussier about heat","It is hardier and more bitter, with about twice the caffeine","It is grown mainly at high altitude for delicate flavour","It is caffeine-free and used mostly for decaf"], 1, "Robusta is tougher, more bitter and earthy, and has roughly twice the caffeine of arabica."],
    [1, "Why were London coffeehouses nicknamed 'penny universities'?", ["Students there were taught for a penny","Professors ran them to raise money","A cup's price bought hours of news and debate","They trained merchants in business skills"], 2, "For the price of a cup you could read newspapers and argue with strangers for hours, and coffeehouses became hubs of trade and ideas."],
    [2, "What does drying the whole cherry in the sun, called natural processing, tend to add?", ["Fruity, wine-like notes","Clean, bright flavours","A smoky bitterness","A grassy aroma"], 0, "Natural processing can add fruity, wine-like notes, while washed processing gives cleaner, brighter flavours."],
    [3, "Which reaction, also browning toast and seared steak, builds much of coffee's flavour in roasting?", ["Fermentation of the beans' sugars","Photosynthesis in the hot bean","The Maillard reaction","Evaporation of the caffeine"], 2, "Amino acids reacting with sugars in the Maillard reaction creates many of roasted coffee's aroma compounds."],
    [4, "A coffee tastes sour and thin. What has most likely happened?", ["The grounds were over-extracted","The beans were roasted too dark","The water carried too much caffeine","Too little was extracted from the grounds"], 3, "Acids dissolve first, so pulling too little from the grounds leaves coffee sour and thin."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Coffee was once banned by rulers who feared what people said over a cup."});

WB.L("chocolate-cacao", "food", "beginner", "Chocolate: from bitter drink to sweet bar",
  "For thousands of years, chocolate was a frothy, bitter drink for rulers and gods. It became the solid, sweet bar we know only in the last two centuries.",
  [
    ["Food of the gods", "For thousands of years cacao was a bitter, frothy drink in the Americas, not a sweet bar.",
      "Chocolate begins with the cacao tree, Theobroma cacao, meaning 'food of the gods'. It is native to the tropical Americas, and traces of cacao on pottery in Ecuador are more than 5,000 years old. The Olmec, Maya and Aztec peoples made it central to their cultures. They ate no chocolate bars. They ground roasted beans into a paste, mixed it with water, often added chilli, maize or flowers, and poured the drink from a height to raise a prized foam. It was bitter, not sweet, and featured in ceremonies, feasts and marriages. The Aztecs valued the beans so highly that they used them as money."],
    ["Sugar, slavery and farms", "Europe's taste for sweet chocolate was tied to colonial plantations, and the industry still struggles with poverty and child labour.",
      "Spanish invaders met cacao in the sixteenth century and took it home. Europeans found the bitter drink strange until it was sweetened with sugar and spiced with cinnamon and vanilla. It became a luxury for royal courts and the wealthy. Because sugar and cacao came from colonial plantations, the taste was tied to the forced labour of enslaved people.\n\nCacao needs hot, humid land near the equator. Today most grows in West Africa, with Cote d'Ivoire and Ghana producing well over half of global supply, mostly on small family farms. Many farmers earn very little, and investigations have repeatedly found child labour. Certification schemes help, with mixed results. When harvests failed in 2023 and 2024, prices hit records and bars grew dearer or smaller."],
    ["How the bar was invented", "A few nineteenth-century inventions turned bitter cacao into smooth, solid chocolate.",
      "Modern chocolate is surprisingly recent. In 1828 the Dutch van Houten family patented a press that squeezed much of the fat, cocoa butter, from ground cacao, leaving a powder that mixed easily into drinks. The cocoa butter proved useful. In 1847 the English firm J. S. Fry and Sons combined cocoa powder, sugar and extra cocoa butter into a mouldable paste, often called the first modern chocolate bar. In the 1870s Swiss maker Daniel Peter used condensed milk, developed by his neighbour Henri Nestle, to create milk chocolate. Soon after, Rodolphe Lindt invented the conche, a machine that stirs chocolate for hours or days, making it far smoother than the gritty bars before it."],
    ["Pod, bean and temper", "Fermenting the beans builds chocolate flavour, and tempering gives the finished bar its gloss and snap.",
      "Cacao pods grow straight from the trunk, each holding dozens of beans in sweet white pulp. Farmers pile the beans and pulp, often under banana leaves, for several days. Yeasts and bacteria feast on the pulp, the pile heats up, and chemical changes build chocolate flavour. Without fermentation cacao tastes astringent and flat. The beans are dried, roasted, cracked and winnowed into nibs, which are ground into cocoa mass. It is also called cocoa liquor, but contains no alcohol.\n\nCocoa butter can set into several crystal forms, and only one gives a glossy, snapping bar. Tempering, heating, cooling and gently reheating, forms the right crystals. Poorly tempered chocolate may show white or grey bloom, which is cocoa butter or sugar on the surface. It looks unappealing but is safe to eat."],
    ["Health, treats and pets", "Chocolate is best enjoyed as a treat, and it is poisonous to dogs.",
      "Cacao contains flavanols, studied for possible benefits to blood vessels, and theobromine, a mild stimulant related to caffeine. Headlines sometimes call chocolate a health food. In reality most chocolate is high in sugar and fat, and processing can strip out many flavanols, so enjoy it as a treat. This is general information, and healthdirect is a good place for dietary advice.\n\nOne warning matters. Dogs break down theobromine far more slowly than people, so chocolate can poison them. Dark chocolate and cooking chocolate are the most dangerous. If a dog eats chocolate, call a vet promptly, especially around Easter and Christmas."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did the Maya and Aztecs usually enjoy cacao?", ["As a sweet solid bar shared at feasts","As a baked biscuit made with maize","As a bitter, frothy, spiced drink","As raw beans eaten straight from the pod"], 2, "Cacao was ground and mixed with water, often with chilli or maize, then poured to make a foamy, bitter drink."],
    [1, "What is true of cacao farming in West Africa today?", ["Many farmers earn little, and child labour has been found","Most farms are huge, fully mechanised estates run by firms","Farmers are paid record prices in every single year","Cacao is no longer grown in any other region"], 0, "Much cacao comes from small family farms where incomes are low, and investigations have repeatedly found child labour."],
    [2, "What did the 1828 van Houten press make possible?", ["Milk chocolate made with condensed milk","A smoother bar made by stirring for days","The first moulded chocolate bar","A cocoa powder that mixed easily into drinks"], 3, "The press squeezed out much of the cocoa butter, leaving a powder that mixed easily into drinks."],
    [3, "Why are cacao beans fermented after harvest?", ["To make the beans mildly alcoholic","To build their chocolate flavour","To remove the caffeine and theobromine","To soften the shells for easy cracking"], 1, "Yeasts and bacteria in the pile trigger changes inside the beans that develop chocolate flavour."],
    [4, "Why is chocolate dangerous for dogs?", ["They break down theobromine very slowly","They are allergic to the sugar in it","Cocoa butter blocks their stomachs","It contains alcohol that harms them"], 0, "Dogs break down theobromine slowly, so it can build up to toxic levels, especially from dark chocolate."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Chocolate bars are less than 200 years old. Before that, you drank it."});

WB.L("tea-story", "food", "beginner", "Tea: one plant, a thousand cups",
  "Green, black, white and oolong tea all come from the same shrub. The differences lie in what happens to the leaves after picking, and the story of how tea spread reshaped empires.",
  [
    ["One plant, many teas", "All true tea comes from one shrub, Camellia sinensis, and oxidation during processing creates green, black, oolong and white.",
      "Green, black, white and oolong tea all come from one evergreen shrub, Camellia sinensis. Peppermint, chamomile and rooibos are herbal infusions, or tisanes, not true tea. The Chinese variety has small leaves and suits cool mountains. The Assam variety, wild in north-east India, has big leaves and gives many black teas their malty strength.\n\nThe real difference is oxidation. After picking, enzymes react with oxygen, as a cut apple turns brown. For green tea, leaves are heated at once, by steaming in Japan or pan-firing in China, keeping them green and grassy. Black tea is withered, rolled and fully oxidised, deepening colour and briskness. Oolong is partly oxidised. White tea is young buds, simply withered and dried."],
    ["China, Japan and Europe", "Tea has been valued in China for over 2,000 years and became an art before it reached Japan and Europe.",
      "Legend says the emperor Shennong discovered tea when leaves blew into his boiling water, but that is a myth. The hard evidence is still old: tea leaves buried in the tomb of a Han dynasty emperor who died in 141 BCE show tea was valued over 2,000 years ago. At first it was often a medicine. In the eighth century, in the Tang dynasty, Lu Yu wrote 'The Classic of Tea', and tea became an art. Buddhist monks carried it to Japan, where powdered green tea, matcha, became the heart of the tea ceremony.\n\nDutch traders brought tea to Europe in the early 1600s. Catherine of Braganza, a Portuguese princess already fond of tea, made it fashionable in England after marrying Charles II in 1662."],
    ["Tea, empire and a heist", "Tea shaped politics, from taxes and silver shortages to Britain's move of production from China to India.",
      "English tea was expensive and heavily taxed, so smuggling flourished. As prices fell, sugared tea became a British staple. In 1773 Boston protesters dumped East India Company tea into the harbour, feeding the American Revolution. Britain bought so much Chinese tea that it ran short of silver, a problem that helped lead to the Opium Wars.\n\nBritain wanted its own supply. Plantations began in Assam, where the local tea variety was identified in the 1820s and 1830s. Between 1848 and 1851 the Scottish botanist Robert Fortune, sometimes in disguise, smuggled thousands of plants and seeds out of China, along with tea-making knowledge and skilled workers. Plantations spread across India and Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, and made tea cheap, but relied on poorly paid labourers in harsh conditions. China remains the biggest producer."],
    ["Brewing a better cup", "Different teas need different water temperatures and steeping times.",
      "Black tea suits water just off the boil and a steep of three to five minutes. Green tea is more delicate: boiling water can scorch it and pull out bitterness, so many people use water cooled to roughly 70 to 80 degrees Celsius and steep it for less time. Oolongs often reward several short infusions from the same leaves.\n\nTea usually has less caffeine per cup than coffee, plus an amino acid called L-theanine, which some research links to a calm, focused feeling. Tea bags spread widely in the twentieth century. A popular story says they began when a New York merchant sent samples in small silk bags, though the history is fuzzier."],
    ["Tea in Australia", "Tea was Australia's everyday drink for most of its history, and it remains popular beside coffee.",
      "For most of Australia's colonial and early national history, tea was the drink of choice. It travelled well and was easy to brew over a campfire, and the billy, a simple tin can for boiling water, became an icon of bush life. Tea was included in rations for convicts, soldiers and workers, and for much of the twentieth century Australians drank far more tea than coffee. Most tea is imported, but small amounts grow in the wet tropics of far north Queensland. Cafe espresso has since overtaken it, yet tea is still hugely popular, with specialty tea shops, bubble tea bars and Chinese and Indian traditions giving it fresh life."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What mainly makes black tea different from green tea?", ["It comes from a different species","It is grown in the dark","It is always flavoured with bergamot","Its leaves are allowed to oxidise fully"], 3, "Black tea leaves are oxidised fully, while green tea is heated early to stop oxidation."],
    [1, "How was tea first used in China?", ["Mainly as a dye for cloth","Often as a medicine at first","Only as an offering at funerals","As a flavouring for rice"], 1, "Tea was often treated as a medicine at first, and only later, in the Tang dynasty, did it become an art."],
    [2, "How did Britain end China's hold on tea?", ["By banning Chinese ships from its ports","By buying all of China's plantations","By switching its drinkers to herbal infusions","By growing its own tea in colonial India"], 3, "Britain started plantations in India with Assam plants, then smuggled out Chinese plants and know-how."],
    [3, "Why do many people brew green tea with water cooler than boiling?", ["Boiling water can scorch it and draw out bitterness","Hot water removes all caffeine","It makes the tea turn black","Green tea will not dissolve in hot water"], 0, "Green tea is delicate, so slightly cooler water gives a smoother, less bitter cup."],
    [4, "Why did tea suit life in colonial Australia?", ["It was grown on most farms near the bush","It travelled well and was easy to brew over a campfire","It was only available to officers and officials","It was brewed in cafes using espresso machines"], 1, "Tea travelled well and was simple to brew over a fire, which is why the billy became an icon of bush life."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Green, black and oolong come from one plant. What changes them?"});

WB.L("australian-bush-foods", "food", "beginner", "Bush foods: Australia's native pantry",
  "Long before supermarkets, this continent was a pantry of seeds, fruits, nuts and greens known intimately by First Nations peoples. Many are now finding their way onto restaurant menus.",
  [
    ["Food knowledge and flour", "First Nations peoples built deep knowledge of native foods over tens of thousands of years, including early seed grinding.",
      "Early Europeans assumed the land offered little to eat. They were badly mistaken. Over tens of thousands of years, First Nations peoples learned which plants and animals were edible, when they ripened, how to prepare them and how to look after the places they grew. That knowledge differed from Country to Country and was tied to seasons, ceremony, law and trade. Bush foods were never lost to the communities who knew them.\n\nIn the dry inland, seeds were winnowed, ground between stones, mixed with water and baked as cakes in hot ashes. Grinding stones tens of thousands of years old are among the oldest evidence of seed grinding anywhere. That suggests bread-making long before farming began in the Middle East."],
    ["Native flavours", "Roasted wattleseed, lemon myrtle and mountain pepper bring distinctive native flavours to kitchens.",
      "Wattleseed comes from certain acacias, the wattles whose golden flowers are Australia's floral emblem. Roasted and ground, it tastes nutty, like coffee, chocolate and hazelnut, and turns up in bread, ice cream and desserts. Only particular species are used for food and some acacias are toxic, so do not experiment alone. Lemon myrtle is a Queensland rainforest tree whose leaves are packed with citral, the compound behind lemon scent. Many people find it more lemony than lemon itself, and it flavours teas, biscuits, marinades and cheesecakes. Pepperberry and pepperleaf, from Tasmanian mountain pepper, add a hot, spicy bite that some cooks use instead of black pepper."],
    ["Fruits and greens", "Finger limes, quandongs, Kakadu plums and warrigal greens are prized for flavour and nutrition.",
      "The finger lime grows wild in rainforest near the New South Wales and Queensland border. Slice one open and tiny pearls of juice tumble out, earning the nickname 'citrus caviar'. Chefs love it with seafood. The quandong, or desert peach, has tart red flesh used in jams and pies, and its hard seed was used for jewellery and games. The Kakadu plum, or gubinge, from northern Australia, has one of the highest vitamin C contents of any fruit, and Aboriginal communities have harvested it for generations.\n\nWarrigal greens grow along coasts and were eaten by Captain Cook's crew to ward off scurvy. They contain oxalates, so blanch them briefly before eating."],
    ["Nuts and gatherings", "Macadamia was commercialised overseas first, while bunya nuts drew people together for great seasonal gatherings.",
      "The macadamia is Australia's most successful native food crop. Its trees come from rainforest in south-east Queensland and northern New South Wales, where Aboriginal people had long eaten the nuts. Ironically, the first big industry grew in Hawaii in the twentieth century, from Australian trees, and many assumed the nut was Hawaiian. Genetic research suggests most Hawaiian commercial trees descend from a very small number of Queensland trees. That narrow base makes the crop vulnerable to disease, and several wild species are now threatened.\n\nIn the Bunya Mountains of Queensland, bunya pines drop huge cones of starchy nuts. In heavy years many Aboriginal groups travelled long distances to feast, hold ceremonies, trade, arrange marriages and settle disputes."],
    ["Respect and first tastes", "Buy bush foods from reputable, ideally Indigenous-owned, suppliers rather than foraging.",
      "The native food industry has grown quickly, but research has found that only a small share of it is owned or led by First Nations people, even though it rests on their knowledge. Many communities and organisations are working to change that, through Aboriginal-owned businesses, fair partnerships and protections for traditional knowledge. Look for products that name the communities involved or come from Indigenous-owned enterprises.\n\nForaging without expert knowledge is risky, because some native plants are toxic or need careful preparation. Buy from reputable suppliers, and learn from First Nations guides where possible. For an easy first taste, try a pinch of wattleseed in pancakes or lemon myrtle in a cup of tea."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do very old Australian grinding stones suggest?", ["Wheat was grown here 50,000 years ago","Stones were only used for weapons","People ground seed flour here very early","Bread arrived with the First Fleet"], 2, "Grinding stones tens of thousands of years old are among the oldest evidence of seed grinding anywhere."],
    [1, "Why is it unwise to try wattleseed from just any wattle?", ["Only some species are food, and some acacias are toxic","Wattle seeds are always unpleasantly bitter to taste","Roasting turns the seeds of every species poisonous","The seeds have no flavour unless they are imported"], 0, "Only certain acacia species are food, and some are toxic, so buy wattleseed from reputable suppliers."],
    [2, "Which native fruit is known for one of the highest vitamin C contents of any fruit?", ["Finger lime","Quandong","Pepperberry","Kakadu plum"], 3, "The Kakadu plum, or gubinge, is famous for its very high vitamin C."],
    [3, "Why is the Hawaiian macadamia crop vulnerable to disease?", ["Hawaii's soil is too poor for nut trees","Most trees descend from very few Queensland trees","The trees are grown far from any rainforest","Wild trees have been crossed with other nuts"], 1, "A narrow genetic base makes the crop more vulnerable, so protecting wild macadamias matters."],
    [4, "What is sensible advice for trying bush foods?", ["Forage any berry that looks ripe and tasty","Taste unknown leaves before cooking them","Buy from reputable suppliers","Avoid native foods completely"], 2, "Some native plants are toxic or need preparation, so buy from reputable sources or learn from First Nations guides."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Australian seed bread may predate farming in the Middle East. Here's the evidence."});

WB.L("fermentation-microbes", "food", "intermediate", "Fermentation: cooking with microbes",
  "Bread, cheese, yoghurt, soy sauce, kimchi, vinegar and Vegemite all depend on tiny living helpers. Fermentation is one of humanity's oldest and cleverest food technologies.",
  [
    ["Controlled rotting", "Fermentation is microbes transforming food in useful ways, steered by salt, temperature, moisture and oxygen.",
      "Leave fruit on the bench and microbes will spoil it. Fermentation is what happens when we steer that process. By controlling salt, temperature, moisture and oxygen, cooks encourage helpful microbes and keep harmful ones out. The helpful microbes eat sugars and other nutrients and produce by-products such as alcohol, carbon dioxide and acids. These change flavour and texture, and often help food last longer.\n\nPeople fermented food for thousands of years without knowing microbes existed. Chemical traces in pottery from Jiahu in China suggest people brewed a drink from rice, honey and fruit about 9,000 years ago. In the 1850s and 1860s, Louis Pasteur showed that living microorganisms are responsible, transforming our understanding of food and disease."],
    ["Yeast, bubbles and Vegemite", "Yeast turns sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide, giving us wine, beer, bread and Vegemite.",
      "Yeast, a single-celled fungus, feeds on sugar and, with little oxygen, produces alcohol and carbon dioxide. Brewers and winemakers want the alcohol. Wine is fermented grape juice. For beer, barley is malted and mashed so its starch breaks into sugars. Bakers want the gas: bubbles of carbon dioxide are trapped in dough's stretchy gluten network, so the loaf rises, and the little alcohol made bakes off. Sourdough uses a living starter of wild yeasts and bacteria, giving a tangier loaf.\n\nFermentation also turns waste into food. Vegemite, created in Melbourne in the early 1920s by chemist Cyril Callister, is made from spent brewer's yeast left over from beer making, mixed with salt and vegetable extracts."],
    ["Bacteria make acids", "Lactic acid bacteria make yoghurt, sauerkraut and kimchi, while acetic acid bacteria turn alcohol into vinegar.",
      "Lactic acid bacteria convert sugars into lactic acid, which makes food tangy and creates acidity where many spoilage and disease-causing microbes struggle. For yoghurt, specific bacteria are added to warm milk. As acid forms, milk proteins tighten and the milk thickens. Sauerkraut and kimchi rely on bacteria already on the vegetables. Salting cabbage draws out water and favours salt-tolerant lactic acid bacteria. Red kimchi is newer than many assume, since chillies only reached Korea from the Americas a few centuries ago.\n\nVinegar is a two-stage fermentation. Yeast first turns sugars into alcohol, making wine, cider or beer. Then oxygen-loving acetic acid bacteria convert that alcohol into acetic acid. 'Vinegar' comes from the French for 'sour wine'."],
    ["Moulds, koji and cheese", "Moulds such as koji make soy sauce and miso, and bacteria, rennet and moulds together make cheese.",
      "Some fermenting microbes are moulds. In East Asian cooking the key one is koji, Aspergillus oryzae. Grown on steamed rice, soybeans or barley, it produces powerful enzymes that break starches into sugars and proteins into amino acids. That starts soy sauce, miso, mirin and sake. Soy sauce is made by mixing koji-covered soybeans and wheat with brine and fermenting for months. The glutamate released gives these foods their savoury umami taste.\n\nCheese begins when bacteria sour milk, which is then curdled, usually with rennet, an enzyme that clumps milk proteins. The whey is drained and the curds salted, pressed and often aged, while bacteria and moulds break down fats and proteins. Moulds also give blue cheeses their veins and brie its white rind."],
    ["Preserving and home safety", "Fermented foods lasted through winters and voyages, but home fermenting needs clean equipment and tested recipes.",
      "Before refrigerators, acid, alcohol and salt made life hard for harmful microbes, so fermented foods kept for months. Communities relied on them through winters and lean seasons, and sailors at sea. On his eighteenth-century voyages, including the one that reached Australia's east coast in 1770, James Cook carried large quantities of sauerkraut, partly hoping to prevent scurvy.\n\nHome fermenting is popular. Use clean equipment, get the salt or acidity right, and keep air out when the recipe says to. Healthy ferments smell sour, yeasty or pleasantly funky. Fuzzy coloured mould, slime or a rotten smell means throw it out. Low-acid foods stored without oxygen can risk botulism, so follow tested recipes. Evidence for gut-health benefits is still developing. See Food Standards Australia New Zealand and healthdirect."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is fermentation in essence?", ["Letting food rot until it becomes edible","Heating food to destroy all microbes","Adding preservatives to stop microbes growing","Steering microbes to change food in useful ways"], 3, "Cooks control salt, temperature, moisture and oxygen so helpful microbes thrive and harmful ones are kept out."],
    [1, "What makes bread dough rise?", ["Lactic acid from the flour","Acetic acid from the starter","Carbon dioxide trapped in the gluten","Salt drawing water into the dough"], 2, "Yeast produces carbon dioxide, which gets trapped in the gluten network and lifts the dough."],
    [2, "Why is salt added when making sauerkraut?", ["It favours salt-tolerant lactic acid bacteria","It turns the cabbage alcoholic","It kills every bacterium present","It gives the cabbage its red colour"], 0, "Salt draws out water and gives salt-tolerant lactic acid bacteria an advantage over other microbes."],
    [3, "Why is koji so useful for soy sauce and miso?", ["It supplies the salt that preserves them","Its enzymes make sugars and amino acids","It adds the bubbles that make them rise","It turns sugars into vinegar"], 1, "Koji's enzymes release sugars and amino acids such as glutamate, which build savoury flavour."],
    [4, "Why should home fermenters follow tested recipes?", ["Fermented foods never keep without a fridge","Tested recipes make clean equipment unnecessary","Untested recipes cannot ever turn sour","Low-acid foods without oxygen can risk botulism"], 3, "Botulism is rare but dangerous, so reliable recipes matter, along with clean equipment and the right salt or acidity."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Vegemite is made from what beer brewers throw away. Fermentation explains it."});

WB.L("spice-trade", "food", "intermediate", "The spice trade: flavours that built empires",
  "Pepper, cloves and nutmeg were once worth fighting and dying for. The hunt for spices sent ships around the world, made fortunes and caused terrible violence.",
  [
    ["Rare, far and precious", "For most of history spices were luxuries because they grew in few places and passed through many hands.",
      "A jar of nutmeg costs a few dollars today, but spices were once luxuries because they grew in so few places. Black pepper came from Kerala's Malabar Coast, true cinnamon from Sri Lanka, cloves from a handful of northern Moluccan islands, and nutmeg and mace almost entirely from the tiny Banda Islands. Indian, Arab, Malay, Chinese and other merchants carried them, each adding a markup.\n\nThe trade is ancient. Romans loved pepper, and merchants used the monsoon winds to cross from Egypt's Red Sea ports to India in weeks. According to ancient accounts, Alaric demanded thousands of pounds of pepper when he besieged Rome in 408. A popular belief says spices hid the taste of rotting meat. Most historians reject it: spices often cost far more than fresh meat."],
    ["Race for a sea route", "Europeans sailed around the world to reach spice markets directly and cut out the middlemen.",
      "In the medieval period, spices reached Europe through Middle Eastern routes and Italian cities such as Venice, which grew rich as middlemen. Other European powers wanted to cut them out. Portugal pushed down the coast of Africa, and in 1498 Vasco da Gama rounded the Cape of Good Hope and reached Calicut in India by sea. Spain sponsored Christopher Columbus, who sailed west in 1492 hoping to reach Asia. He reached the Caribbean instead, where he met chillies and allspice. He called the chillies 'peppers', a name that has confused people ever since. Ferdinand Magellan's expedition, which completed the first circumnavigation of the globe in 1522, was also largely a search for a route to the spice islands."],
    ["Monopolies and massacres", "The Dutch East India Company used extreme violence to control nutmeg and cloves.",
      "In the seventeenth century the Dutch East India Company, the VOC, set out to control the most valuable spices completely. Its methods were brutal. In 1621 forces under governor-general Jan Pieterszoon Coen conquered the Banda Islands, the world's only real source of nutmeg. Most Bandanese were killed, enslaved or driven away, and the islands became plantations worked by enslaved people. The Dutch also restricted clove trees to particular islands and destroyed trees elsewhere to keep prices high.\n\nWhen England and the Netherlands made peace in 1667, the Dutch kept Run, a tiny nutmeg island the English had held, while England kept New Amsterdam, which it had seized and renamed New York. In hindsight, the deal looks rather different."],
    ["How spices got cheap", "Smuggled seedlings and changing tastes ended the monopolies and made spices ordinary.",
      "Monopolies over living plants rarely last. In the 1770s a French official, Pierre Poivre, organised expeditions that smuggled clove and nutmeg seedlings out of the Dutch-controlled islands to Mauritius and other French colonies. During the Napoleonic Wars the British also moved spice plants to places such as Ceylon and Penang, and nutmeg later reached Grenada, which still has one on its flag. Clove growing became a major industry on Zanzibar, worked by enslaved people.\n\nAs spices were grown in more places, prices fell. European tastes also shifted towards sugar, coffee, tea and chocolate. Spices once worth a fortune became ordinary kitchen staples, sold cheaply in jars all over the world."],
    ["Saffron, vanilla and legacy", "The priciest spices today are labour-intensive, and the trade left a complicated legacy that still shapes our food.",
      "Some spices stay expensive because they take so much work. Saffron, usually the priciest by weight, is the thread-like stigmas of a crocus. Each flower has only three, picked by hand, so a kilogram needs well over a hundred thousand flowers. Iran grows most of it, with small amounts from Tasmania. Vanilla is the cured pod of a Mexican orchid. Elsewhere its natural pollinators are missing, until in 1841 Edmond Albius, a twelve-year-old enslaved boy on Reunion, devised the quick hand-pollination method still used today.\n\nThe trade moved plants, people and ideas. Chillies became central to Indian, Thai, Sichuan and Korean food, and pepper sits beside salt on most Western tables. It also left a legacy of conquest, monopoly, slavery and environmental change."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do most historians reject the idea that spices hid the taste of rotten meat?", ["Spices were banned in medieval Europe","Rotten meat was never eaten","Spices often cost far more than fresh meat","Spices were only used in drinks"], 2, "Spices were expensive status goods, prized for flavour, medicine and status, so using them to rescue spoiled meat makes little sense."],
    [1, "Why did European powers want a sea route to Asia?", ["To buy spices directly at the source","To escape the monsoon winds","To trade with the Americas first","To avoid paying for Indian pepper"], 0, "Venice and Middle Eastern traders profited as middlemen, so Portugal and Spain sought direct routes to the source."],
    [2, "How did the Dutch East India Company try to keep spice prices high?", ["By sharing cloves and nutmeg freely with rivals","By taxing spices heavily when they reached Europe","By buying up all of Europe's spice stocks","By holding the islands and destroying trees elsewhere"], 3, "The VOC conquered the Banda Islands and restricted or destroyed trees elsewhere to hold its monopoly."],
    [3, "What helped make spices cheap and ordinary?", ["Europeans stopped liking strong flavours","Plants were smuggled out and grown in many places","Spices became easier to make in factories","Ships began sailing faster"], 1, "Once seedlings were smuggled out and grown elsewhere, the monopolies broke and prices fell."],
    [4, "Why are saffron and vanilla still expensive?", ["They only grow in one country","Governments restrict their sale","They need a lot of skilled hand labour","Their flavours fade within days"], 2, "Saffron stigmas are picked by hand and vanilla flowers are hand-pollinated, so the work is costly."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"New York was once traded for a tiny nutmeg island. Here's why."});

WB.L("umami-msg", "food", "intermediate", "Umami and the MSG story",
  "A Japanese chemist tasting his soup discovered a fifth basic taste. The seasoning he created went on to become one of the most misunderstood ingredients in the world.",
  [
    ["A chemist and his soup", "In 1908 Kikunae Ikeda identified a savoury taste he named umami, then created MSG.",
      "In 1908 the Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda was enjoying dashi, a broth made with kombu seaweed, when he wondered about its distinctive savoury taste. It was not sweet, sour, salty or bitter, the four tastes scientists recognised then. Working with large quantities of kombu, he isolated crystals of glutamic acid, an amino acid, and showed that its salts carried the taste. He named the sensation umami, from a Japanese word for delicious, roughly 'savoury deliciousness'. Ikeda then found a stable, easy-to-use seasoning based on it, monosodium glutamate, or MSG. With a business partner, he launched it in Japan in 1909 as Ajinomoto."],
    ["Glutamate is everywhere", "Glutamate occurs naturally in many foods, and certain compounds multiply its umami effect.",
      "MSG sounds industrial, but glutamate is one of nature's most common amino acids. Our bodies make it, and breast milk contains it. Usually it is locked inside proteins, where we cannot taste it. When proteins break down through ripening, ageing, drying, cooking or fermenting, free glutamate is released. That is why ripe tomatoes taste more savoury than green ones, and why parmesan, soy sauce, fish sauce, miso, mushrooms, anchovies and Vegemite are so rich in depth.\n\nOther Japanese scientists later found inosinate in dried bonito and guanylate in dried shiitake. Combined with glutamate, these nucleotides multiply umami rather than adding to it. Japanese dashi pairs kombu with bonito. Western cooks pair tomatoes or parmesan with meat."],
    ["A fifth taste confirmed", "Dedicated taste receptors confirmed umami as the fifth basic taste, probably a signal for protein.",
      "For decades many Western scientists doubted that umami was a separate taste. They argued it was a blend of other tastes, or an effect of smell. The debate shifted in the early 2000s, when researchers identified taste receptors on the tongue that respond specifically to glutamate, giving umami a clear biological basis. Today it is widely accepted as the fifth basic taste, alongside sweet, sour, salty and bitter.\n\nWhy would we evolve to detect it? A common explanation is that umami signals protein. Glutamate is released when protein-rich foods are cooked or aged, so a taste that rewards it steers us towards nourishing food. Sweetness signals energy from sugars, bitterness may warn of toxins, and sourness of unripe or spoiled food."],
    ["How a rumour began", "A 1968 letter started decades of fear about MSG, but the evidence does not support it.",
      "For its first half-century MSG drew few complaints. In 1968 a doctor wrote to a prominent medical journal describing numbness and palpitations after eating at Chinese restaurants in the United States, wondering whether cooking wine, sodium or MSG was to blame. The term 'Chinese restaurant syndrome' spread through newspapers, and restaurants posted 'no MSG' signs. Many now see the panic as tinged with prejudice, since parmesan, tomatoes and stock were rarely blamed.\n\nSince the 1970s, many studies, including double-blind trials, have generally failed to find consistent reactions at normal food amounts. Food Standards Australia New Zealand considers MSG safe for the general population. In Australia it appears on labels by name or as flavour enhancer 621, and 'no added MSG' products may still contain natural glutamate. This is general information, so speak with a doctor about any concerns."],
    ["Cooking with umami", "A little umami adds depth and can even help cut the salt in a dish.",
      "Today MSG is mostly made by fermentation. Bacteria feed on sugars from crops such as sugarcane, corn or cassava and produce glutamate, which is purified and crystallised. It looks like fine white salt, tastes mild on its own, and lifts soups, stir-fries, stews and dressings. MSG has roughly a third as much sodium as table salt, so swapping some salt for a little MSG can reduce sodium without losing flavour.\n\nYou do not need the crystals to cook with umami. A splash of soy or fish sauce, a spoonful of tomato paste, a grating of parmesan, a few dried mushrooms or a dab of Vegemite in gravy all deepen flavour in the same way."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Kikunae Ikeda's discovery show about savoury taste?", ["It is just a blend of salty and sweet","It is a distinct taste linked to glutamate","It comes only from seaweed","It is an effect of smell alone"], 1, "Ikeda isolated glutamic acid from kombu and named its savoury taste umami, separate from the four known tastes."],
    [1, "Why does dashi, made with kombu and bonito flakes, taste so strongly savoury?", ["The two ingredients cancel out any bitterness","Bonito flakes contain added MSG","Boiling releases sugar from the seaweed","Glutamate and inosinate multiply each other's umami"], 3, "Nucleotides such as inosinate multiply glutamate's umami effect rather than simply adding to it."],
    [2, "Why might humans have evolved to detect umami?", ["It signals protein-rich food","It warns of spoiled food","It signals sugary food","It marks foods high in salt"], 0, "Glutamate is released when protein-rich foods are cooked or aged, so umami may steer us towards nourishing food."],
    [3, "What does the evidence say about MSG at normal food amounts?", ["Studies show it causes serious harm","Double-blind trials found no consistent reactions","It is banned in Australia","Natural glutamate is safe but MSG is not"], 1, "Trials have generally failed to find consistent reactions, and Food Standards Australia New Zealand considers MSG safe."],
    [4, "How can a cook add savoury depth while cutting salt?", ["Use more salt and less umami","Add a lot of sugar to mask saltiness","Swap some salt for a little MSG","Boil the dish for much longer"], 2, "MSG has roughly a third of table salt's sodium, so a small swap can lower sodium without losing flavour."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A chemist tasted his soup in 1908 and found a taste nobody had named."});

WB.L("chilli-heat", "food", "advanced", "Why chillies burn: the science of heat",
  "Chilli heat is not a taste at all. It is a clever chemical trick that fools your pain sensors, and the story of how it conquered the world's kitchens is just as surprising.",
  [
    ["From Mexico to the world", "Chillies are American plants that spread around the globe within a century of Columbus's voyages.",
      "Thai, Indian, Sichuan and Korean food all learned to use chillies only in the last five centuries. Chillies belong to the genus Capsicum, originated in the Americas and were domesticated in Mexico thousands of years ago. After Columbus's voyages, Spanish and especially Portuguese traders carried them around the world. Unlike many crops, they were easy to grow from seed, thrived in warm climates and offered a cheap, intense flavour. Within about a century they grew in India, Africa and South-East Asia, where local cooks made them their own, often replacing or supplementing black pepper."],
    ["How capsaicin fools you", "Capsaicin triggers the nerve receptor that detects painful heat, so chilli burns without any actual heat.",
      "The burn comes mainly from capsaicinoids, chiefly capsaicin. Taste buds do not detect it. Instead it binds to TRPV1, a protein on pain-sensing nerve endings. TRPV1 normally detects dangerous heat, switching on above roughly 43 degrees Celsius. Capsaicin flips the same switch, so your brain concludes your mouth is burning though the temperature has not changed. That is why chilli can make you sweat and flush. Work on how such receptors sense temperature and pain earned David Julius a share of the 2021 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\n\nIn 1912 pharmacist Wilbur Scoville measured heat by diluting extract until tasters felt none. Labs now measure capsaicinoids directly and convert to Scoville units. A bell pepper scores zero, a jalapeno a few thousand, and the hottest bred varieties exceed two million."],
    ["Why plants burn us", "Capsaicin deters mammals but not seed-spreading birds, and we enjoy the burn as a safe thrill.",
      "Why would a plant make something that burns? One explanation is directed deterrence. Mammals chewing chillies crush the seeds, while birds swallow them whole and spread them in droppings. Birds' TRPV1 is insensitive to capsaicin, so they eat the hottest chillies in comfort. Capsaicin may also protect fruit from fungi: wild chillies in wetter parts of Bolivia, where fungal attack is more common, tend to be hotter. Scientists still debate how much each factor matters.\n\nHumans are among very few animals that seek out the burn. Psychologist Paul Rozin calls it benign masochism, like enjoying a roller coaster while your brain knows you are safe. Endorphins may add pleasure, though how much is uncertain. Tolerance builds, since repeated exposure makes nerve endings less responsive for a while."],
    ["Where the heat hides", "Most capsaicin sits in the pale ribs, not the seeds, and it dissolves in fat rather than water.",
      "Many people think the seeds are the hottest part. In fact capsaicin is made mostly in the pale, spongy ribs, or placenta, that hold the seeds. Seeds taste hot because they press against it. Removing the ribs and seeds tames a chilli considerably.\n\nCapsaicin is oily. It dissolves poorly in water but well in fats and alcohol, so gulping water mostly spreads it around. Dairy such as milk and yoghurt helps, probably because its fat and a protein called casein help carry capsaicin away. Starchy foods like rice or bread can also give some relief by mopping up the oil. Handle very hot chillies with gloves, avoid touching your eyes, and take extreme chilli challenges cautiously. Capsaicin is also the active ingredient in some pain-relief creams and in pepper spray."],
    ["Other kinds of fire", "Pepper, wasabi and Sichuan pepper each trigger different sensations from chilli's burn.",
      "Not every pungent spice works like chilli. Black pepper gets its bite from piperine, which also activates TRPV1 and a related receptor, though more mildly. Wasabi, horseradish and mustard rely on isothiocyanates, which are volatile, so the heat rushes up the nose and fades quickly. They activate a different receptor, TRPA1, which also responds to irritants such as the fumes from cut onions.\n\nSichuan pepper, from the dried husks of a prickly ash shrub unrelated to chillies or black pepper, does something stranger. A compound called hydroxy-alpha-sanshool produces a tingling, buzzing numbness that researchers have compared to a mild vibration on the lips. Sichuan cooking pairs this numbing sensation with chilli heat in mala, meaning 'numbing and spicy'."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did chillies spread so quickly after 1492?", ["They were easy to grow and full of flavour","They were rare and kept as royal luxuries in Europe","They could only be grown in cool regions","They were used as a replacement for sugar"], 0, "Chillies grew easily from seed in warm climates and offered a cheap, intense flavour, so cooks everywhere adopted them."],
    [1, "How does capsaicin make your mouth feel hot?", ["It raises the temperature of the tongue","It switches on a heat-pain receptor","It destroys the sweet taste buds","It dissolves the lining of the mouth"], 1, "Capsaicin flips the same TRPV1 switch as real heat, so the brain gets a burning message though nothing is hotter."],
    [2, "Why can birds eat very hot chillies without discomfort?", ["They have no taste buds","They only eat the seeds","Their stomach acid destroys capsaicin instantly","Their version of TRPV1 is insensitive to capsaicin"], 3, "Birds' TRPV1 does not respond to capsaicin, so they eat chillies and spread the seeds intact."],
    [3, "Which is the best way to ease a burning mouth after chilli?", ["Drink a large glass of cold water","Eat more chilli","Drink milk or eat yoghurt","Rinse with fruit juice"], 2, "Capsaicin dissolves in fat, so dairy's fat and casein help carry it away, while water mostly spreads it around."],
    [4, "How does the heat of wasabi differ from chilli's?", ["It lingers longer on the tongue","Volatile compounds rush up the nose and fade quickly","It comes from the same compound as chilli","It numbs the lips instead of burning"], 1, "Wasabi's isothiocyanates are volatile and activate TRPA1, so the heat hits the nose and fades fast."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Chilli heat isn't a taste at all. Your mouth is being tricked."});

